{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Remember what HaShem your Elohim did to Miriam.</b> This is a positive commandment concerning the matter of evil speech (lashon ha-ra). It says: we are to remember the evil speech of Miriam and the punishment that befell her because she spoke against Moses, and the punishment of leprosy fell upon her. Therefore this verse is joined to the verse above that says, ״Take heed regarding the plague of leprosy.״ If so, the verse is a warning against evil speech, and it is a negative prohibition derived from an inclusive positive command." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to learn from this a fortiori (kal va-chomer) the greatness of the punishment that awaits those who tell evil speech: if Miriam the righteous woman, the prophetess, who spoke only concerning her brother, and she was older than he and had raised him and risked her life for him at the Nile, and she spoke not in his presence — for all that, so great a punishment came upon such speech, and none of her merits availed her against the punishment; and moreover the speech she spoke was not evil speech in the strict sense but only that she made him equal to other prophets — how much more so for other people who tell actual evil speech about those greater than they, and to their faces so as to shame them, that their punishment will be doubled and redoubled. And because of this the Torah forbade associating with fools and those who sit at the street corners, for they speak idle talk, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 5): ״וקול כסיל ברוב דברים״ — and the voice of a fool is through a multitude of words; and from such idle talk they will come to tell evil speech about the masses of the people, and from speaking about the masses they will come to speak about the righteous, as it is written (Psalms 31): ״תאלמנה שפתי שקר הדוברות על צדיק עתק״ — let lying lips be silenced, which speak presumptuously against the righteous; and from this they will come to speak about the prophets, as it is said (II Chronicles 36): ״ויהיו מלעיבים במלאכי האלהים ובוזים דבריו ומתעתעים בנביאיו״ — and they mocked the messengers of Elohim and despised His words and scoffed at His prophets; and from this they will come to speak about the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Psalms 73): ״שתו בשמים פיהם״ — they set their mouth against the heavens, etc. What caused them to set their mouth against the heavens? Their tongue that walks in the earth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:53.755660474Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}